Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Lines wider than 1 pixel in an OpenGL core profile: the standard line
programs get a geometry stage that draws each segment as a screen-space
quad of the line's width (mitcad#2063, GitHub mitcad/Mitcad#440).

Expected: a line aspect of width w is drawn w pixels wide (times the
context's line width scale), as in a compatibility profile.
Actual: in a core profile every line is 1 pixel wide, whatever its width.
OpenGl_Context::SetLineWidth calls glLineWidth only on OpenGL ES or when
core11ffp is set, and core11ffp is null in a core profile; a
forward-compatible core context (the only one with shaders on macOS)
does not draw lines wider than 1 pixel through glLineWidth in any case.
Reproducer: an OpenGl_GraphicDriver with contextCompatible off (a core
profile), an edge displayed with a Prs3d_LineAspect of width 4, the view
dumped: the edge is 1 pixel wide (observed with Mesa llvmpipe, OpenGL 4.5
core, on a virtual X display, and in MitCad's macOS view, OpenGL 4.1
core).

- Graphic3d_ShaderFlags_WideLine (0x4000, so Graphic3d_ShaderFlags_NB is
  0x8000; Graphic3d_ShaderFlags_NeedsGeomShader includes it): a program
  that draws lines as quads.
- Graphic3d_ShaderManager: the geometry stage of such a program takes a
  line segment (layout lines in, a triangle strip of 4 vertices out:
  Graphic3d_ShaderObject::CreateFromSource with 2 input vertices). It
  clips the segment by the near plane and draws a quad occLineWidth
  pixels wide around it in window coordinates, extended by half the
  width at each end so that the segments of a polyline join without
  gaps, counter-clockwise. The quad's z and w are the segment's, so its
  depth is the line's. Each corner passes on the vertex outputs of its
  end of the segment; the fragment stage is the line program's (colour,
  the stipple pattern from gl_FragCoord along the line's major axis,
  clipping planes). The lit programs take the same stage.
- OpenGl_ShaderManager::ToExpandWideLines(width): desktop OpenGL without
  the fixed-function pipeline (core11ffp null, ffpEnable off) and with a
  geometry stage, and the width times LineWidthScale above 1 pixel.
  BindLineProgram takes the width (1 by default) and sets the flag then.
  Custom programs, width 1, compatibility profiles and OpenGL ES are
  unchanged.
- OpenGl_Context::SetLineWidth sets occLineWidth (the width times the
  line width scale) on the active program where it does not call
  glLineWidth.
- OpenGl_PrimitiveArray::Render passes the aspect's line width to
  BindLineProgram for GL_LINES and GL_LINE_STRIP. While a wide line
  program draws, it turns polygon offset off (a line aspect's default
  Aspect_POM_Fill would push the quads back as it pushes faces, level
  with the faces the line lies on), face culling off and the polygon
  mode to filled, and restores them after.
- Edges of faces drawn with the polygon mode GL_LINE (drawEdges: dashed
  mesh edges, no geometry stage, or usePolygonMode) stay 1 pixel wide in
  a core profile; the mesh edges' geometry stage already draws solid ones
  at their width.

Test: none in geometry/tests, because the defect exists only in a
core-profile context, which the OCCT-only tests do not create. The test
is app.ui-macos-selection-look (tools/ui-scripts/selection-look.mitcad-ui,
its view-line-width steps): it reads the macOS view's pixels back, a
selected edge at least 3 logical pixels wide at Normal and wider at
Thick, a sketch line at least 2 (less the driver's half pixel). Checked
with OCCT alone in a core profile (Mesa llvmpipe, OpenGL 4.5 core):
widths 4 and 6 drawn 4 and 6 pixels wide (1 without the patch), width 1
unchanged, a dashed line of width 4 dashed, a line on a shaded face at
its depth drawn over it and one under the face hidden.

Upstream: new; changes only lines wider than 1 pixel in a desktop core
profile, which were drawn 1 pixel wide; their ends are extended by half
the width.

